+// `tracker` and `geom_tracker` are both self-referential (`PropertyTracker` registers a pointer
+// to its own address into every property it reads), so each must never move while alive. Bundling
+// them into one heap allocation - instead of a separate `Pin<Box<PropertyTracker>>` each - halves
+// the per-item allocation count while staying sound: the *pointer* to this box is free to move
+// (e.g. when `slab::Slab`'s backing `Vec` reallocates on growth), only the pointee must not.
+// `data` deliberately stays outside this box and inline in `PartialRenderingCachedData` itself
+// (read/written on every cached item every frame, regardless of whether that item's trackers are
+// ever touched) so it keeps living in the slab's own contiguous, cache-friendly storage rather
+// than behind a per-item pointer chase.
+#[pin_project::pin_project]
+struct PartialRenderingCachedDataTrackers {
+    /// The property tracker that should be used to evaluate whether the item needs to be re-rendered.
+    /// Lazily created (starts `None`) the first time the item is actually rendered - see `do_rendering`.
+    #[pin]
+    tracker: Option<PropertyTracker>,
+    /// Tracks the properties read while computing `data` (geometry, clips_children,
+    /// children_transform, and whatever the item's own `bounding_rect()` reads). While this
+    /// stays clean, `compute_dirty_regions` can skip recomputing `data` for this item and its
+    /// dependency registration entirely, reusing the previous value instead.
+    #[pin]
+    geom_tracker: PropertyTracker,
+}
+
